# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Skip and the consequence rule" (second part).
id: skip-not-valid
title: A Skip triple that cannot be proved
topic: Skip and consequence
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Skip and the consequence rule"
tags: skip, consequence, counterexample

task:
  Explain why the following triple cannot be proved, and give a value of
  $x$ that shows that it is not valid.

pre: x > 0
post: x > 5

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  skip        // i1
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of skip
rule: SKIP, WP
focus: i1
text:
  `skip` does not change the state. By the Skip rule, its weakest
  precondition is its postcondition, here the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(skip, Q)
  = x > 5       // wp(skip, Q) is Q
set: R1 = x > 5

step: P implies R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  $R1$ is the weakest precondition of the program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R1$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: x > 0 ==> x > 5
status: fails
counterexample: x = 1
why:
  The state $x = 1$ satisfies $P$, since $1 > 0$, but not $R1$, since
  $1 > 5$ is false. So the consequence rule cannot be applied, and the
  triple cannot be proved. The triple is also not valid: `skip` does not
  change the state, so the program ends with $x = 1$, and $Q$ is false.
result: The triple is not valid. The state $x = 1$ is a counterexample.

summary:
  For `skip`, the weakest precondition of $Q$ is $Q$ itself, so the
  triple is valid exactly when $P ==> Q$. Here $x > 0$ does not imply
  $x > 5$, and every value of $x$ from $1$ to $5$ is a counterexample.
